+**The energy split with the splat (added 2026-08-27).** The gather layers used to carry each
+texel's FULL colour while the sprite pass *additionally* deposited its excess above `Highlight
+Threshold`. A gather is a normalised average, but over a stratified kernel it still spreads a
+point's whole light L over its disc in expectation, so a lamp came out at L (gather) + (L - T)
+(sprite): up to **twice** its real brightness, with the error a function of a purely aesthetic
+threshold. The lamp's full L inside the gather is also what made the tap copies so visible - the
+brightest thing in the frame was the thing being sampled 121 times.
+
+The frame's energy is therefore split once, in the **downscale**, before the 5-tap average:
+
+```
+base_i  = c_i * (L_i - handover * excess(L_i)) / max(L_i, 1e-4)      // hue preserving
+excess  = the sprite's OWN soft knee (quadratic over [T-k, T+k], linear above), shared code
+handover = splatActive * smoothstep(fade*0.333, fade, CoC)           // the sprite's own fade
+```
+
+so that `base + sprite == the texel` by construction. Three things make it exact rather than
+approximate:
+
+* **The knee is one function** (`LensHighlightExcess` in the .cfx, `ResolveHighlightKnee` on the
+  CPU). Two authored copies of the same derivation would let the two halves of the hybrid stop
+  summing to the frame.
+* **Per sample, not per average.** `max(0, x - t)` is convex, so the excess of a mean understates
+  the mean of the excesses; the sprite already thresholds its four native texels individually for
+  the same reason. The downscale takes 5 bilinear taps and the sprite 4 native texels of the same
+  cell - per-sample clamping is the consistent choice on both sides.
+* **The handover follows the sprite's own fade and cull.** A highlight whose disc is too small to
+  be splatted keeps its excess in the gather, or it would be given away to nobody. The downscale
+  reads this pixel's own CoC while the sprite reads the once-dilated tile copy, which is never
+  smaller: any disagreement leaves a sliver of excess in the gather rather than counting it twice.
+* **With the splat off (`r_DepthOfFieldMode 1`) `splatActive = 0`** and the gather keeps every
+  texel whole, because then it is the only thing carrying light.
+
+On screen: with the threshold below a lamp's luminance the gather's underlay of that lamp is dim
+(at most T) and the lamp is essentially the sprite alone. Total brightness no longer depends on
+where the threshold sits.
+
 **Near-layer opacity** (what the composite blends the near layer over the rest with) is the
 pupil-weighted fraction of rays that land on the foreground:
 
 ```
-α_near = Σ_i cov_i · P_i · isNear_i  /  Σ_i P_i        // isNear_i = tap's near CoC > 0.01
+α_near = Σ_i cov_i · P_i · isNear_i · (R / c_i)²  /  Σ_i P_i    // isNear_i = tap's near CoC > 0.01
 ```
 
+**The density weight `(R / c_i)²` (added 2026-08-27) is not cosmetic.** The taps are spread over
+a disc of radius `R = fNearReach`, which is the tile **maximum** CoC, dilated along the tile
+chain. A foreground point of CoC `c_i` spreads its light over `π c_i²`, not `π R²`, so counting
+covering taps as a fraction of all taps in the reach disc measures the pupil fraction *only* when
+`c_i == R`. Whenever the tile maximum came from a closer object or from the dilation at an edge -
+which is most of the time - the count is low by `(c_i/R)²` and the foreground composites in too
+transparent. `c_i` here is the same capped, floored copy the colour weights use
+(`max(min(coc, cap), 0.125)`); the denominator is unchanged and the result still saturates.
+Sanity: a uniform foreground at `c_i = R` is unchanged; at `c_i = R/2` the quarter as many taps
+that still cover are each worth four times as much, so α lands where it would if `R` had been
+`c_i`.
+
 written to the near layer's alpha in lens mode **instead of** the averaged CoC. In a uniformly
 blurred foreground every tap covers, α = 1; at a blurred foreground edge over a sharp
 background α falls off as the true fraction of the pupil that sees the foreground — the partial
@@ -222,7 +271,27 @@ jittered tap is clamped back to the iris polygon's radius at its own angle, whic
 in `g_Taps[].z` (re-deriving it in the shader costs an `atan2` and a `pow` 242 times a pixel).
 The kernel as a whole is never rotated or translated — that would soften the polygon's outline,
 which is exactly the part worth keeping exact; only the interior is roughened. TSAA resolves the
-noise. `r_DepthOfFieldLensModelJitter 0` restores the bare grid for A/B.
+noise.
+
+**Temporal phase (added 2026-08-27).** `IGN_pixel` depends on the pixel alone, which makes the
+jitter *fixed-pattern* noise — and fixed-pattern noise is the one thing temporal AA cannot average
+away: every frame it re-accumulates the identical error, so the taps never converge and the whole
+point of the jitter is lost. Both lanes therefore take a per-frame golden-ratio phase,
+`frac(IGN + frac(frame · (0.618034, 0.381966)))`, with the frame counter
+(`SPostEffectsUtils::m_iFrameCounter`, wrapped at 1024 so it stays an exact float) arriving in
+`vDofLensModel2.z`. Each frame hands TSAA a different member of the sample set, which is what
+turns 121 taps into a smooth disc instead of 121 stable copies.
+
+**Mask domain (added 2026-08-27).** When the gather runs the mask CDF kernel, `g_Taps[].z` is 1 —
+the bounding circle — because a mask silhouette has no radius function, so the shader's clamp back
+to the aperture does nothing and a full-amplitude jitter can push a tap out of the mask's open area
+into its closed one, smearing the silhouette the mask kernel exists to keep. The CPU therefore
+quarters the amplitude in that domain (`vGather0Lens2.y *= 0.25`) and says so on the `[DOF] kernel`
+line.
+
+`r_DepthOfFieldLensModelJitter 0` restores the bare grid for A/B; `2` keeps full amplitude but
+freezes the frame phase (the old static per-pixel pattern), which is how tap structure is told
+apart from TAA ghosting.
 
